---
// The landing page: phase 8's eleven sections in order. The headline and paragraph are final; every
// other line is draft copy for phase 8 to rewrite.
import Install from './_Install.astro'
import Page from './_Page.astro'
import { faq, guides, paneEntry, tour } from './_landing'
import { href, wireframe } from './_nav'
---

<Page title="Landing">
  <main>
    <section class="wrap section" style="border-top: 0">
      <p class="tag">1 · Hero</p>
      <p class="tag">Eyebrow, draft: FOR DEVELOPERS WHO RUN CODING AGENTS</p>
      <h1>A workspace for working with coding agents, one task at a time.</h1>
      <p>
        Open a task and acorn gives it a branch, a Git worktree, terminals, and agent sessions, with the diff and
        the pull request in the panes beside them. Use it from the desktop app or from your terminal. It's the same
        workspace either way.
      </p>
      <div class="row">
        <a class="button primary" href={wireframe('download/no-release')}>Get acorn</a>
        <a class="button" href={wireframe('docs-home')}>Read the docs</a>
      </div>
      <Install />
      <p>Desktop app for macOS and Windows · terminal client · command line · browser extension</p>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section">
      <p class="tag">2 · The picture</p>
      <figure class="stack">
        <div class="row"><span class="button">Desktop</span><span class="button">Terminal</span></div>
        <div class="box" style="aspect-ratio: 16 / 10">
          Shell snapshot, full width: one task with an agent session, the Changes pane, and the terminal drawer.
          Drawn from HTML text, not an image.
        </div>
        <figcaption>The same task in the desktop app and in the terminal client.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section">
      <p class="tag">3 · How it works</p>
      <div class="cols" style="--min: 280px">
        <div class="stack">
          <h2>1. Open a task</h2>
          <p>A task is one piece of work on one project. It gets a branch, and a Git worktree the first time it needs files.</p>
          <div class="box" style="block-size: 200px">Specimen: the new task form</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <h2>2. Run agents in it</h2>
          <p>Start Claude Code or Codex sessions in the task. Answer their approvals, queue turns, and hand work to child sessions.</p>
          <div class="box" style="block-size: 200px">Specimen: an agent turn waiting on an approval</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <h2>3. Review and ship</h2>
          <p>Read the diff, open the pull request, and merge from the same window. Archive the task and acorn cleans up its worktree.</p>
          <div class="box" style="block-size: 200px">Specimen: the pull request's checks and merge button</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section">
      <p class="tag">4 · The tour</p>
      <h2>What's in a task</h2>
      <div class="row">
        {tour.map((name, index) => <span class:list={['button', { primary: index === 0 }]}>{name}</span>)}
      </div>
      <div class="cols" style="--min: 320px">
        <div class="box" style="aspect-ratio: 16 / 10">Shell snapshot for the selected tab</div>
        <div class="stack">
          <p>
            Workspaces group projects, and a project holds tasks. Each task keeps its own row of panes, its terminals,
            and its agent sessions, so switching tasks puts you back where you left that work.
          </p>
          <a href={wireframe('features')}>More about tasks</a>
          <p class="tag">Without script, every tab's panel shows, in this order.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section">
      <p class="tag">5 · Two clients, one workspace</p>
      <h2>The desktop app and the terminal client draw the same panes</h2>
      <p>Both clients draw the same components, so a plugin written once works in both.</p>
      <div class="cols" style="--min: 320px">
        <div class="box" style="block-size: 320px">Specimen, desktop view: a plugin pane</div>
        <div class="box" style="block-size: 320px">Specimen, terminal view: the same pane, 80×24</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section">
      <p class="tag">6 · Add what's missing</p>
      <h2>Add a pane with a manifest entry</h2>
      <div class="cols" style="--min: 320px">
        <div class="code">
          <div><span>acorn-plugin.json</span><span>Copy</span></div>
          <pre set:text={paneEntry} />
        </div>
        <div class="box" style="min-block-size: 240px">Specimen: the pane that entry draws</div>
      </div>
      <ul class="cols" style="--min: 200px; padding: 0; list-style: none">
        {
          guides.map(([base, title]) => (
            <li>
              <a href={href(base, title)}>{title}</a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>Or ask your agent to write it. acorn's agents read the plugin vocabulary from your Node.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section">
      <p class="tag">7 · Your machine, your keys</p>
      <h2>What agents and plugins can't reach</h2>
      <div class="cols" style="--min: 240px">
        <a class="card" href="/docs/security/the-trust-model/">Agents get a token scoped to their task. They never see your credentials.</a>
        <a class="card" href="/docs/security/credentials-and-secrets/">Credentials are encrypted at rest and left out of backups.</a>
        <a class="card" href="/docs/security/plugin-trust-and-permissions/">Installed plugins run sandboxed, with only the permissions you accept on each device.</a>
        <a class="card" href="/docs/security/telemetry-and-privacy/">Telemetry is off by default.</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section">
      <p class="tag">8 · Run it anywhere</p>
      <div class="cols" style="--min: 320px">
        <div class="stack">
          <h2>Your Node, on this machine or another</h2>
          <p>
            The desktop app runs a Node on your machine. Pair others, including a standalone Node on a server, and use
            them from the desktop app, the terminal client, or the command line.
          </p>
          <a href="/docs/nodes/">Read about Nodes</a>
        </div>
        <div class="box" style="block-size: 260px">Small shell snapshot: Fleet home with two Nodes</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section">
      <p class="tag">9 · FAQ</p>
      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <div class="stack">
        {
          faq.map(([question, answer]) => (
            <details>
              <summary>{question}</summary>
              <p>{answer}</p>
            </details>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section">
      <p class="tag">10 · Install again</p>
      <h2>Get acorn</h2>
      <Install />
      <div class="row">
        <a class="button primary" href={wireframe('download/no-release')}>Get acorn</a>
        <a class="button" href={wireframe('docs-home')}>Read the docs</a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <p class="wrap tag">11 · Footer, below</p>
  </main>
</Page>
